INT. FATHER'S TACKLE SHOP - EVENING

Rows of rods in wall racks, tins of hooks, a stuffed pike on a plaque. A bell above the door has gone still. The shop smells of old rope and kerosene.

CALLIE FENWICK-NAKAMURA, forties, in a canvas jacket, stands at the counter with a cardboard box. She is packing a life's work into it. A pair of reading glasses. A thermos. A ledger. She works slowly, as if the objects might break if she hurried.

On the wall, a landline phone with a long curled cord. Beside it, an answering machine blinks 1.

She stops. Looks at the machine. Looks away. Picks up the thermos. Sets it down. Her hand drifts to the button and then retreats.

She goes to the window, flips the sign from OPEN to CLOSED, and rests her forehead on the cold glass.

The machine clicks. A tape whirs.

CALLIE (V.O.)
(recorded greeting)
Hi, you've reached Fenwick's Tackle. We're out on the water. Leave a message and we'll call you back when the fish quit biting.

A BEEP.

She turns around. Slowly. Crosses to the counter. Presses the button again.

CALLIE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded greeting)
Hi, you've reached Fenwick's Tackle...

She presses stop. The tape clicks. She stands with her palm flat on the machine, the way one touches a sleeping animal.

Then, briskly, she wraps the cord around her wrist and pulls the phone off the wall. A cloud of plaster dust drifts down. She sets the phone in the box. Folds the flaps. Tapes them shut.

The bell above the door rings. She does not look up. She reaches for the stuffed pike on its plaque and takes it down, runs a finger along its glass eye, and, to the empty shop, in a voice almost too quiet to hear:

CALLIE
(recorded greeting)
We'll call you back.

She tucks the pike under her arm. Picks up the box. Turns off the light.
